-- title:
--   The t-line of the Tate torsion parametrisation is cyclic
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a nontrivially normed, ultrametric, complete field of characteristic zero which is algebraically closed, and let $q,\zeta,t\in K$ with $q\neq 0$ and $\|q\|<1$. Let $p$ be a prime with $p\ge 5$, let $\zeta$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $t^{p}=q$. Then for every natural number $j<p$ one has, in the group of affine points of the Tate curve $E_q$ over $K$ — the Weierstrass curve with coefficients $(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6)=(1,0,0,a_4(q),a_6(q))$ given by the usual $q$-series — the identity $$\mathrm{tateTorsionPoint}(0,j)=j\cdot\mathrm{tateTorsionPoint}(0,1).$$ Here [`TateCurve.tateTorsionPoint`](def/TateCurve_TorsionParametrization.html#L905) assigns to a pair of indices $(i,j)$ with $i<p$, $j<p$ and $(i,j)\neq(0,0)$ the affine point whose coordinates are the convergent series $\mathrm{pointX}(q,\zeta^{i}t^{j})$ and $\mathrm{pointY}(q,\zeta^{i}t^{j})$ (each a sum over $\mathbb{Z}$ of the corresponding $x$- or $y$-terms, corrected by the Eisenstein-type quantity $s_1(q)$), together with a proof of nonsingularity, and assigns the zero point to all other index pairs; thus the asserted equality reads, for $0<j<p$, as the statement that the point at parameter $t^{j}$ is $j$ times the point at parameter $t$, and for $j=0$ as $0=0$.
--
--   This is the $t$-direction half of the multiplicativity of the Tate parametrisation $K^{\times}/q^{\mathbb{Z}}\to E_q(K)$ restricted to the $p$-torsion parameters, i.e. the classical statement that the parameter line $\{t^{j}\}$ maps onto the cyclic subgroup generated by the image of $t$ (compare Silverman, Advanced Topics, Thm. V.3.1). It is used, alongside the corresponding statement for the $\zeta$-line, to establish additivity of the parametrisation in [`TateCurve.tateTorsionEquiv_add`](thm.html#TateCurve.tateTorsionEquiv_add).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_TateCurve_tateTorsionPoint_snd_eq_nsmul.lean

